  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 PM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nThe upper-level low center is currently tracking northeast \nacross Eastern Idaho, with distinct closed circulation visible \non both satellite and radar imagery. Wrap-around precipitation \nwill persist across the region through tonight as the system \nslowly ejects toward the Northern Rockies, supporting periods of\nmoderate to locally heavy rainfall rates. The main \nprecipitation focus through this evening will concentrate over \nthe Snake Plain and the southern/eastern highlands, while \nactivity across the central mountains gradually diminishes. \nConsequently, the flash flood threat over the Wapiti burn scar \nhas ended, allowing the Flood Watch to expire at noon; however, \nthe Flood Watches for the Ohio and Big Rock burn scars remain in\neffect through late afternoon due to ongoing vulnerability.\n\nAlthough guidance has trended toward slightly better consensus,\nnotable ensemble spread persists regarding additional QPF\nthrough tonight, with the bulk of accumulation occurring before\nmidnight. NBM 25th percentile totals remain around 0.10 inches\nin the central mountains, scaling up to near 1.00 inch along the\nContinental Divide and Island Park. Meanwhile, 90th percentile\nvalues reach 1.00\u20131.50 inches across the Snake Plain/highlands\nand up to 2.00 inches along the Divide. Official QPF closely\ntracks NBM 50th percentile/mean values (roughly half of the 90th\npercentile) with the understanding that a few locations could\nsee the higher amounts materialize under the influence of\nheavier bands. Snow levels may briefly drop toward valley floors\nin some areas overnight before precipitation tapers off, though\naccumulations will remain restricted to mid-slope elevations and\nabove, keeping travel impacts negligible. Dry, stable conditions\nunder a transient high-pressure ridge will prevail Saturday\nthrough Monday before the next system approaches by mid-week\nwith significantly lighter QPF.",
